# Things `docker build` should not copy into the image.
#
# Same idea as .gitignore, different question. .gitignore asks "should this be
# in version control?"; this asks "does the RUNNING APP need this?". A file can
# easily be a yes to the first and a no to the second -- the test suite, for
# instance.
#
# Two reasons to bother: a smaller image builds and boots faster, and anything
# left out cannot be served by accident.
#
# DELIBERATELY CONSERVATIVE. Only files nothing imports are listed. It is
# tempting to drop draft_model/ too, since the draft pages are hidden on a
# public deploy -- do not. `app_context.py` imports DraftSimService at module
# level, so the app would fail to start with an ImportError before it ever got
# as far as deciding which pages to show.

# Version control. Copying it in would add the entire project history to the
# image for no reason.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# Regenerable caches and OS junk
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.DS_Store

# Draft-simulation artifacts: ~81 MB of .npz files, and already untracked by
# git. Only the Sim Viewer reads them, and that page is hidden on any
# deployment -- see deployment.py.
data/sim/

# The modelling sandbox. `research/` reads the app but the app never reads it,
# so leaving both out cannot break the container. data/research/ is Parquet
# snapshots, which can run to hundreds of MB.
notebooks/
research/
data/research/
requirements-research.txt

# Tests, notebooks and documentation. All useful; none of it runs in the
# container.
tests/
docs/
*.ipynb
# The line above only matches notebooks at the top level -- Docker's `*` does
# not cross a folder boundary. This one catches them in any subfolder.
**/*.ipynb
*.md
!README.md

# Local tooling
.claude/
.agents/
Developer Notes/
.venv/
venv/
